# GitHub pull sync

Stephen and Chris share one codebase across two GitHub copies. **Pull before you edit. Pull before you commit. Push both remotes after you commit.**

## Remotes

- `origin` / `stephen` = `https://github.com/StephenZepeda/ai-video.git`
- `chris` = `https://github.com/ChrisZepeda147/ai-video.git`

## Start of session (before editing shared code)

Run this when the user opens the repo or asks you to work on code, web, scripts, or config:

```powershell
git fetch origin
git fetch chris
git status -sb
```

If `main` is behind `origin/main`:

```powershell
git stash push -u -m "WIP before pull sync"
git pull --rebase origin main
git stash pop
```

If stash pop conflicts, stop and resolve before continuing.

**Both machines** — pull shared library packages (Stephen + Chris):

```powershell
python scripts/shared_library_sync.py pull-import
```

Or `python scripts/shared_library_sync.py install` once — Windows timer every 5 min pulls + pushes source on both remotes.

## Before every commit

1. Run the pull steps above so the commit sits on latest `origin/main`.
2. Do not commit on a branch that is behind remote without pulling first.

## After every shared commit

Push both remotes. Do not leave one GitHub copy stale.

```powershell
git push origin
git push chris
```

## If remotes are missing

```powershell
git remote add stephen https://github.com/StephenZepeda/ai-video.git
git remote add chris https://github.com/ChrisZepeda147/ai-video.git
```

## Do not

- Skip pull because "we were just working here" — the other machine may have pushed.
- Push only `origin` after a shared code commit.
- Force-push `main` to fix sync drift.

